   "variant_id": null,
   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "A function \\( f \\) has derivatives of all orders. The second-degree Taylor polynomial for \\( f \\) about \\( x = 0 \\) is used to approximate \\( f\\left(\\frac{3}{10}\\right) \\). It is known that \\( f'''(0) = 5 \\) and that \\( |f'''(x)| \\le 10 \\) for all \\( x \\) in \\( \\left[0, \\frac{3}{10}\\right] \\). The approximation is meant to be within \\( \\frac{1}{10} \\) of \\( f\\left(\\frac{3}{10}\\right) \\). Find the Lagrange error bound for this approximation."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Rational",
         9,
         200
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "A degree 2 polynomial needs the maximum of \\( |f'''(x)| \\), the derivative of order \\( 3 \\), between the centre and the input.",
         "mathjson": 3
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "The bound is \\( \\frac{M\\,|x - 0|^{3}}{3!} \\) with \\( M = 10 \\) and \\( |x - 0| = \\frac{3}{10} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            9,
            200
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Evaluating gives \\( \\frac{10 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{10}\\right)^{3}}{6} = \\frac{9}{200} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            9,
            200
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "The error is at most \\( \\frac{9}{200} \\), which is at most \\( \\frac{1}{10} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            9,
            200
         ]
      }
   ],
